  "analysis_narrative": "The humidity model reveals a powerful inverse relationship with temperature (coefficient = -2.4, p < 0.001) and significant regional effects. Sidi Bel-abbes maintains approximately 7.2% lower humidity than Bejaia across all temperatures, suggesting fundamentally different microclimates. The model explains roughly 62% of humidity variance (R-squared = 0.62), with residuals generally satisfying normality assumptions. For the FFMC interaction model, fire-affected locations have substantially higher baseline FFMC (+76.8 points) but demonstrate reduced temperature sensitivity (slope decreases from 2.4 to 0.5). This counterintuitive finding suggests that in fire-prone areas, fuel moisture reaches a floor level beyond which temperature becomes less influential -- possibly due to consistently dry vegetation or different forest composition. The quadratic humidity model further illuminates non-linear dynamics, with FFMC decreasing acceleratively as humidity rises above 35%. This curvilinear relationship outperforms linear alternatives and identifies a transitional zone around 35-50% humidity where the protective effect of moisture begins to accelerate sharply. The FWI component model confirms that ISI and BUI together explain approximately 92% of FWI variance, with ISI being the dominant standardized driver of fire weather intensity."
